{"event_id":"EVT-000082","timestamp_utc":"2026-08-24T12:51:41.952Z","session_id":"SESSION-BATCH-20260824T084007661319Z","run_id":"RUN-IMO-108-20260824T124836062464Z","requirement_id":"IMO-108","event_type":"validator_feedback_possible_reversal","iteration":4,"prior_feedback_history":["Remove the unconditional sh:minCount 1 constraints on the conditional inputs, especially separation, capacities, category, and construction date; they incorrectly require applicability evidence and the regulated separation for every ship. Keep datatype/unit constraints conditional on present values and let the SPARQL constraint determine applicability from the supplied category/date/capacity values. Also revise the violation query so an applicable case fails when separation is absent or when any reported separation is below 0.76 m; the current FILTER NOT EXISTS passes accidentally when one qualifying separation exists alongside an inadequate one.","Preserve the prior conditional-cardinality repair: do not add property-level sh:minCount 1 for category, construction date, capacities, or separation; the nested cardinalities may remain to validate each supplied QuantityValue. Repair the applicable-case SPARQL violation logic: an applicable ship must fail when oilFuelTankOuterShellSeparation is absent, and must fail when any reported separation is below 0.76 m. Replace the current NOT EXISTS separation OR EXISTS inadequate-separation expression with an existence requirement combined with NOT EXISTS for an inadequate reported separation, so absence cannot pass accidentally.","The prior repair is still not reflected in the SPARQL constraint. Preserve the conditional property cardinality approach, but replace the current separation expression with the instructed existence requirement combined with a NOT EXISTS check for an inadequate reported separation, ensuring an applicable ship fails when separation is absent and when any reported separation is below 0.76 m. Do not add unconditional property-level sh:minCount constraints."],"current_feedback":"Remove the unconditional sh:in (nltl:polarShipCategoryA nltl:polarShipCategoryB) from the shipCategory property shape. The canonical range also permits Category C, and this requirement must not reject non-applicable Category C ships; applicability belongs in the conditional SPARQL logic. Preserve the conditional absence handling for category, date, capacities, and separation. Rewrite the separation violation branch explicitly so an applicable ship is selected when no separation is reported or when any reported separation is below 0.76 m, without allowing a qualifying value to mask an inadequate value.","blocking":false,"semantic_attempt_consumed":false}
